I can’t give a reliable numeric probability of guilt from this short summary alone.

Why: this is exactly the kind of situation where the “two infant deaths in one family” fact can be badly misused if treated as simple statistical proof of murder. The right question is not “how rare is SIDS twice?” but “how strongly do all the medical findings, taken together, distinguish homicide from natural or accidental causes?” That requires expert evaluation of:

- whether Ben’s rib/spinal/eye findings are truly specific for inflicted injury rather than resuscitation, birth-related issues, disease, or post-mortem artefact;
- whether Andy’s reinterpreted bruises, torn frenulum, and blood in lung are genuinely diagnostic of smothering/abuse;
- timing of injuries;
- consistency of pathology opinions;
- alternative medical explanations;
- the base rates of double SIDS, homicide, and false-positive forensic interpretations.

That said, if I take the summary at face value, the case against Jane is substantial, because:
- there were two unexplained infant deaths while she was present,
- Ben’s later autopsy reportedly found multiple injuries commonly associated with non-accidental trauma,
- Andy’s materials were later re-read as showing injuries said to be characteristic of abuse/smothering.

But there are still major cautions:
- “characteristic of” is not the same as “conclusive of”;
- retrospective reinterpretation of pathology can be uncertain;
- without knowing the defence medical evidence, I can’t assess how specific these findings really are;
- guilt in law depends on proof beyond reasonable doubt, not just suspicion.

So, on the limited facts given, I would say the evidence points more toward guilt than innocence, but not with high confidence suitable for a real verdict.

62

Taking the evidence as it has evolved in this conversation, the case now looks quite mixed rather than strongly one-sided.

Points supporting guilt:
- two infant deaths in the same family under superficially similar circumstances;
- Ben had hypoxic brain damage and eye/eyelid haemorrhages, which may be consistent with smothering or violent trauma;
- Andy’s findings were later reinterpreted as suggestive of abuse.

Points cutting against guilt:
- Andy’s injuries were initially thought consistent with resuscitation;
- Ben lacked some classic signs of smothering such as petechiae;
- nurses did not note Ben’s injuries on arrival;
- the spinal bleeding may be common in natural death;
- the rib findings may have been post-mortem or due to resuscitation.

Once those defence-style explanations are included, the pathology becomes much less specific for homicide. The repeated deaths remain suspicious, but suspicion is not the same as strong proof. So on the totality of the evidence you’ve now presented, I think guilt is somewhat less likely than not.

